          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where the sources disagree</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>SE4 display size.</strong> Abt's SE4 listing gives a 20.5-inch display in its
            specification table and a 24-inch screen in its description. The 20.5-inch figure is the SL's
            LED panel; Life Fitness lists the SE4 as 24 inches. We use Life Fitness's figure.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty.</strong> Life Fitness and The Fitness Outlet list lifetime cover on the
            frame and motor, 10 years on parts, 3 years on the console and 1 year of labour. Abt lists the
            same lifetime, 10-year and 1-year terms without the console term. BarBend's review is
            inconsistent about whether the motor and the console are covered for life, and adds six
            months on cosmetic items. A UK retailer lists very different terms (three years parts and
            labour, ten years motor and frame), which apply to UK sales only. Get the terms for your
            unit in writing.</li>
            <li><strong>Older prices.</strong> BarBend quotes about $7,900 with the SL, $9,100 with the ST
            and $10,600 with the SE3 HD. Those prices are for consoles Life Fitness no longer listed in
            the US and should not be used to judge a current price.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            One more figure worth knowing: BarBend describes a two-ply belt on 3.5-inch rollers and a
            FlexDeck 2.0 system with eight Lifespring shock absorbers. Life Fitness's store describes
            FlexDeck and Lifesprings but we did not find the roller diameter there, so treat it as one
            source's figure.
          </p>
