        <div id="consoles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. SE4 vs SL: Which Club Series+ Console to Buy</h2>
<p>
            The console is the only choice you make on a new Club Series+, and it is a large one: at Life
            Fitness's sale prices on 5 November 2026 the SE4 cost $2,720 more than the SL, and at regular
            prices $3,400 more. The treadmill underneath is the same.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The SL console</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness describes the SL as a 20.5-inch LED display with 22 preloaded workouts. It has no
            touchscreen and no entertainment apps. BarBend adds Bluetooth connectivity. In practice the
            SL is a clear, simple panel for speed, incline, time and heart rate, with a shelf for your
            own tablet. If you run to a plan, watch your own screen or prefer silence, it is all the
            console you need, and it has less to go wrong. Life Fitness's console warranty is three
            years either way, and a large touchscreen is a more expensive part to replace after that.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The SE4 console</h3>
          <p>
            The SE4 is a 24-inch, 1080p touchscreen. Life Fitness and the dealers list Netflix and Spotify,
            Life Fitness's own On Demand+ classes, Lifescape filmed courses that move with your speed,
            Bluetooth 5.0 connectivity, Polar and ANT+ heart-rate support, <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apple-watch/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Apple Watch</a> and Samsung Galaxy
            Watch compatibility and a Qi wireless phone charger rated at up to 10 W. It is the same console
            family Life Fitness fits to its commercial treadmills, which is part of the appeal: the
            screen you used at the gym, at home.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buy the SE4 if you will use what it adds every week: the Lifescape courses, the classes, or a
            large built-in screen in a room where a tablet would be awkward. Buy the SL if the screen is a
            nice-to-have. A good tablet on the SL's shelf gives you streaming and most training apps for a
            fraction of the difference, and you can replace it every few years without touching the
            treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does the SE4 need a subscription?</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness's store did not state a subscription price for the SE4's content on the day we
            checked, and its listing treats streaming apps as your own accounts. Before buying, ask the
            seller in writing which SE4 features work without any paid Life Fitness plan, and what any
            plan costs after the first year. The SL needs nothing: it runs its preset workouts on its own.
            Our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            explains why that matters over a machine's life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-and-where" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What the Club Series+ Costs and Where to Buy It</h2>
<p>
            Life Fitness sells the Club Series+ directly through its US consumer store and through
            authorised dealers. The prices we saw on 5 November 2026 were these:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Life Fitness store:</strong> from $6,079 with the SL console (regular $7,599) and
            $8,799 with the SE4 (regular $10,999), in a <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Black Friday</a> sale advertised as up to 20% off with
            no end date shown. Free delivery and installation, including room placement and packaging
            removal, were part of the listing.</li>
            <li><strong>The Fitness Outlet:</strong> $6,079 (regular $7,599) on a listing offering both
            consoles without a separate SE4 price.</li>
            <li><strong>Abt:</strong> $10,999 for the SE4 in Black Onyx.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Two things follow. First, the price moves a lot with Life Fitness's sales, so a full-price
            dealer quote in a sale week is worth challenging. Second, the SE4 is where prices vary most:
            $8,799 at one seller and $10,999 at another on the same day. Ask every seller what is included
            — delivery, installation, removal of an old treadmill, and which warranty — before comparing
            totals.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery lead time</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness's product page listed delivery and installation windows of 3-4 weeks and 5-7 weeks
            for different configurations without saying which applied to which. If you need the
            treadmill by a date, ask for the lead time on your exact console and finish.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is it on Amazon?</h3>
          <p>
            We did not find a new Club Series+ sold by Life Fitness on Amazon US. The Amazon button on this
            page goes to a tagged search for Life Fitness treadmills, which mainly returns other Life
            Fitness models and accessories. Buy the Club Series+ itself from Life Fitness or an authorised
            dealer so the warranty and installation are clear.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the CPSC recall database for Life Fitness on 5 November 2026. It lists no recall
            of the Club Series+ or the Platinum Club Series. The only Life Fitness treadmill recall it
            holds is from September 2003 and covered about 2,800 older club treadmills (the 9000HR, 8500
            and T9i, in stated serial ranges) that could accelerate unexpectedly; a 2018 recall covered
            PowerMill climbers, not treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Putting a Club Series+ in Your Home</h2>
<p>
            A Club Series+ is a fixture. It does not fold, and at 434 lb it is not something you move to
            vacuum. Plan four things before you order.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Floor space</h3>
          <p>
            The footprint is 82.25 by 36.25 inches. ASTM's safety guidance for treadmills, which most
            manufacturers repeat, asks for clear space behind the deck (commonly 79 inches, or 2 metres)
            and to each side; Life Fitness's own manual is the authority for your unit. In practice that
            means a room or a garage bay, not a corner of a bedroom. Our
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to putting a gym treadmill in a home</a>
            covers the space, the floor and the access route in detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ceiling height</h3>
          <p>
            The step-up is 8 inches, and at 15% incline the front of the deck rises further. Add your
            height and a few inches of bounce when running. A <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>tall runner</a> in a basement with a 7-foot
            ceiling should measure before buying.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness lists a 120 V, 15 A grounded outlet in North America. That is a practical
            difference from the commercial Integrity+, which needs a dedicated 20-amp circuit. A
            dedicated circuit is still a good idea for any AC treadmill, because sharing a circuit with a
            freezer or a space heater can trip it under load, and treadmill makers generally advise
            against extension cords. Ask the installer to check the outlet before they leave.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Access route</h3>
          <p>
            Abt lists the packaged weight as 562 lb and the box at about 85 by 39 by 42 inches. Life
            Fitness's installation includes room placement, but measure doorways, stair turns and
            landings first; a basement with a tight turn at the bottom of the stairs is the common
            problem. Ask the seller whether the installers will carry it up or down stairs and whether
            that costs extra.
          </p>
        </div>

