          <p>
            <strong>Against the Run CX</strong>, the Club Series+ buys you an AC motor rated a full
            horsepower higher on a continuous basis, a heavier frame, lifetime rather than 10-year motor
            cover and 10 rather than 7 years on parts, plus the option of the SE4 screen. The running
            area, top speed, incline and user rating are identical. If you are one walker or a moderate
            runner, those extras are hard to justify at $2,000 to $5,000 more. If two or three people
            will put many hours a week on it, the motor and parts terms start to matter.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against the Platinum Club Series</strong>, the Club Series+ is the same idea updated.
            A used Platinum Club Series at a deep discount can be a fair buy, but it comes with an older
            console, no transferable warranty and parts that are getting older; see our Platinum Club
            Series page for what to check.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against the Integrity+</strong>, the Club Series+ gives up 2 mph of top speed and some
            commercial durability, and gains a standard outlet, a simpler purchase from Life Fitness's
            consumer store and a lower price. Unless you need 14 mph or are equipping a space that will
            see commercial-level use, the Club Series+ is the better home buy. For a wider look at the
            category, our <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmills guide</a>
